{"passage_id": "AUSTEN_EMMA_019", "condition": "paraphrase", "rewritten_text": "Woodhouse, I wish you had heard the gallant things he said to me at dinner. Oh! I can assure you I began to think my dear husband would be quite jealous. I imagine I am rather favored; he noticed my gown. What do you think of it?—chosen by Selina—handsome, I believe, though I am uncertain if it is too much adorned; I have a strong aversion to the idea of being overdone—truly dislike extravagance in dress. I must wear some ornaments now, as it is expected of me. A bride, you know, must look like a bride, but my natural inclination is for simplicity; a plain style of dress is infinitely preferable to showy finery. Yet I suspect I am in the minority; few seem to appreciate plainness in dress—display and finery are everything. I have an idea of adding a trimming like this to my white and silver poplin. Do you think it will look well?”

  "The entire company had just gathered again in the drawing-room when Mr. Weston appeared among them. He had returned from a late dinner and walked to Hartfield as soon as he was done. He had been so anticipated by the best judges that there was no surprise—but there was great delight. Mr. Woodhouse was almost as pleased to see him now as he would have been sorry to see him before. John Knightley, however, was in silent astonishment.—That a man who might have spent his evening quietly at home after a day's business in London, should set out again and walk half a mile to another man’s house, for the sake of being in mixed company until bedtime, of finishing his day with the exertions of politeness and the din of numbers, was a matter to strike him deeply. A man who had been active since eight o’clock in the morning, and might now have been still, who had been long speaking, and might have been silent, who had been in more than one crowd, and might have been alone!—Such a man, to leave the peace and independence of his own fireside, and on the evening of a cold, sleety April day, rush out again into the world!—Could he by a touch of his finger have instantly taken back his wife, there would have been a motive; but his coming would probably prolong rather than end the gathering. John Knightley looked at him with amazement, then shrugged his shoulders, and said, “I could not have believed it even of _him_.”

  "Mr. Weston, meanwhile, perfectly unaware of the indignation he was causing, cheerful and happy as ever, and with all the right to be the main speaker, which a day spent anywhere away from home bestows, was making himself agreeable among the rest; and having satisfied the inquiries of his wife regarding his dinner, convincing her that none of her careful instructions to the servants had been neglected, and shared what public news he had heard, was proceeding to a family communication, which, though mainly addressed to Mrs. Weston, he had not the slightest doubt of being highly interesting to everyone in the room. He gave her a letter, it was from Frank, and to herself; he had encountered it on his way, and had taken the liberty of opening it."}

