The whole group had just gathered in the drawing-room when Mr. Weston arrived. He had returned for a late dinner and walked to Hartfield as soon as it was over. He had been expected, so there was no surprise—but there was a lot of happiness. Mr. Woodhouse was almost as glad to see him now as he would have been sad to see him before. John Knightley was just stunned. That a man who could have spent his evening quietly at home after a busy day in London should go out again and walk half a mile to someone else’s house just to be with people until bedtime, to spend his evening being polite and in a noisy crowd, was something that shocked him deeply. A man who had been busy since eight in the morning and could now be at rest, who had been talking a lot and could now be quiet, who had been in crowds and could now be alone!—Such a man, to leave the peace and freedom of his own home and on a cold, rainy April evening go out again into the world!—If he could have instantly taken back his wife, there would have been a reason; but his coming would probably make the party last longer. John Knightley looked at him in amazement, then shrugged his shoulders, and said, “I could not have believed it even of _him_.”

Meanwhile, Mr. Weston, completely unaware of the anger he was causing, was as happy and cheerful as usual, and with all the right to be the main speaker, which a day away from home gives you, was making himself pleasant among the others; and having answered his wife’s questions about his dinner, convincing her that none of her careful instructions to the servants had been ignored, and shared what public news he had heard, was moving on to a family announcement, which, though mainly addressed to Mrs. Weston, he had no doubt at all would be very interesting to everyone in the room. He gave her a letter, it was from Frank, and to her; he had found it on his way, and had taken the liberty of opening it."}

{"passage_id": "POE_WORKS_VOLUME_1_003", "condition": "simplify", "rewritten_text": "In about four and a half hours, I found the balloon sufficiently inflated. I attached the car and placed all my tools inside, including the condensing apparatus, plenty of water, and a large amount of food like pemmican, which is very nutritious but takes up little space. I also put a pair of pigeons and a cat in the car. It was almost morning, and I thought it was time to leave. I dropped a lit cigar on the ground as if by accident, and while I bent down to pick it up, I quietly lit the fuse that extended slightly from the bottom of one of the smaller barrels. The three men didn't notice this at all. I jumped into the car, cut the single rope that tied me to the ground, and was happy to see that I rose easily, carrying 175 pounds of lead ballast, and could have carried just as much more.

"However, I had barely reached a height of fifty yards when a terrible storm of fire, smoke, sulfur, legs, arms, gravel, burning wood, and flaming metal came roaring up after me in the most frightening and chaotic way. My heart sank, and I fell to the bottom of the car, shaking with pure fear. I realized then that I had gone too far, and the worst effects of the explosion were yet to come. In less than a second, I felt all the blood rush to my temples, and then a shock, which I will never forget, burst through the night and seemed to tear the sky apart. Later, when I had time to think, I didn't fail to blame the extreme force of the explosion on my position directly above it and in the path of its greatest power. But at the time, I only thought of saving my life. The balloon first collapsed, then violently expanded, then spun around with terrifying speed, and finally, swaying and staggering like a drunk man, it threw me with great force over the edge of the car and left me hanging, at a terrifying height, with my head down and my face outward, by a thin piece of cord about three feet long, which hung accidentally through a crack near the bottom of the wicker, and in which, as I fell, my left foot became very conveniently entangled. It is impossible to imagine the horror of my situation. I gasped for breath, a shudder like a fit of the ague shook every nerve and muscle in my body, I felt my eyes popping from their sockets, a terrible nausea overwhelmed me, and finally I fainted.

"How long I remained in this state is impossible to say. It must, however, have been some time, for when I partially regained my senses, I found the day breaking, the balloon at a great height over an endless ocean, and not a trace of land to be seen far and wide within the vast horizon."}
